{
  "image_name": "0c55523a70d76c14cd9d3f9bebbf006d.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how epidermal cell fate is controlled by epigenetic regulators during the transition from proliferation in the basal layer to terminal differentiation in the upper skin layers. The main panel shows a regulatory network in which factors such as p63, ACTL6A, BRG1, SATB1, GRHL3, TrxG, JMJD3, HDAC1/2, PRC2, DNMT1, and CBX4 either promote or repress differentiation. Differentiation genes, including components of the epidermal differentiation complex and proteins like filaggrin, loricrin, involucrin, and LCE, are activated as cells move upward from the stratum basale through the spinous and granular layers to the stratum corneum. In contrast, proliferation-associated pathways in basal cells involve p16INK4A and p21, with p53, MYC, and SETD8 also linked into this control system.\n\nA key theme is the balance between activating and repressive chromatin states. H3K27me3 appears as a major repressive mark that is removed or opposed during differentiation, while histone acetylation is associated with activation. The smaller summary panel emphasizes this shift: non-epidermal and stem cell genes lose prominence, differentiation genes increase, H3K27me3 marking decreases overall, histone acetylation rises, and expression of HDAC1/2 and CBX4/DNMT1 seems to decline with differentiation.",
